[0.45, "o", "check[ok]: host-platform (required=true) - Local Firecracker launch is available on Linux hosts.\r\ncheck[ok]: machine:demo:volume:data:attached-volume (required=false) - machine 'demo' attached volume 'data' backend 'host-file' persistence 'persistent' host path '/tmp/nix-shell.SlGpx4/port-attached-volume-proof-azd981rb/demo-data.ext4' is ready for launch route 'direct-local-runtime' with inventory owner 'local-runtime-root' and lifecycle owner 'local-port-runtime'.\r\nnote: port doctor reports the host state without mutating runtime directories.\r\nnote: macOS operators can run Port against the AVF lane locally; Firecracker and Firecracker/PVM still require Linux host access.\r\nnote: Windows operators should use WSL or a remote Linux host, then rely on port doctor to confirm whether local Firecracker launch is available.\r\nnote: SSH-managed remote hosts surface separate auth and bootstrap expectations; they do not reuse hosted control-plane bearer-token auth or local runtime ownership.\r\nnote: Remote Linux hosts are modeled provider-by-provider, but the MVP launch path is still local Linux only.\r\nnote: Firecracker/PVM readiness is reported as a dedicated host-kit lane; failing PVM checks do not imply the standard Firecracker lane is a compatible fallback.\r\nnote: Cloud Hypervisor readiness is reported through its own host-platform, architecture, protection-mode, and binary checks; Port does not silently fall back to Firecracker when that lane is selected.\r\nnote: AVF install and help surfaces stay on the canonical port CLI: set PORT_AVF_LAUNCHER to an external launcher helper for local macOS workflows, and do not expect a bundled macOS-only fallback workflow in this slice.\r\n"]
